const MP_RANGES=[{key:"great",name:"Great Himalaya",icon:"🏔️",blurb:"The main axis of the range — the passes here cross the watershed itself."},{key:"pirpanjal",name:"Pir Panjal",icon:"⛰️",blurb:"The outer Himalayan range separating the Kashmir valley and Kullu from the plains."},{key:"zanskar",name:"Zanskar",icon:"❄️",blurb:"Between the Great Himalaya and the Indus, cutting across Ladakh."},{key:"ladakh",name:"Ladakh Range",icon:"🐐",blurb:"North of the Indus, crossed by the roads out of Leh."},{key:"karakoram",name:"Karakoram",icon:"🧊",blurb:"The highest and most remote — the old caravan route to Central Asia."},{key:"eastern",name:"Eastern Himalaya",icon:"🌧️",blurb:"Sikkim, Arunachal and the Bhutan and Tibet frontiers."}],MP_STATUS=[{key:"motorable",name:"Motorable Road",icon:"🚗"},{key:"trek",name:"Trekking Pass",icon:"🥾"},{key:"tunnel",name:"Bypassed by Tunnel",icon:"🚇"},{key:"closed",name:"Closed / Restricted",icon:"🚫"}],MOUNTAIN_PASSES=[{name:"Khardung La",icon:"🏁",localName:"la — pass (Ladakhi)",elevationM:5359,range:"ladakh",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Leh → Nubra Valley and the Shyok",season:"Usually open year-round, with closures after heavy snow",tagline:"The most over-claimed elevation in India",desc:'Khardung La carries the road from Leh into the Nubra valley and is the gateway to Siachen. Signboards at the top have long claimed 18,380 ft (5,602 m) and "world\'s highest motorable road". Neither stands up: modern GPS and survey work put the pass nearer 5,359 m, and several passes in Ladakh are higher. It is still a genuinely high crossing and the road over it is one of the busiest at that altitude anywhere.',history:"Part of the old caravan route from Leh to Kashgar. The road was built in the 1970s and opened to civilian traffic in 1988.",caution:"The climb from Leh gains well over 1,800 m in a short distance. Acute mountain sickness is common here, and the standard advice is to acclimatise in Leh for at least two days first."},{name:"Umling La",icon:"🔝",localName:"la — pass (Ladakhi)",elevationM:5798,range:"ladakh",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Chisumle → Demchok, in eastern Ladakh",season:"Nominally open much of the year; extreme cold and wind at any season",tagline:"The highest motorable road in the world, and this one is documented",desc:"Completed by the Border Roads Organisation in 2021, the road over Umling La reaches roughly 5,798 m — higher than Everest Base Camp on either side. Unlike the older claims made for Khardung La, this one is properly surveyed and is recognised as the highest motorable road anywhere.",history:"Built as border infrastructure in eastern Ladakh, in an area with almost no permanent settlement.",caution:"Oxygen at this altitude is roughly half that at sea level. This is not a casual drive, and the nearest medical facility is a long way off."},{name:"Zoji La",icon:"🚧",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:3528,range:"great",state:"Ladakh / Jammu & Kashmir",status:"motorable",connects:"Srinagar → Kargil → Leh (NH-1)",season:"Typically closed by snow from around December to April",tagline:"The reason Ladakh is cut off for months every year",desc:"Zoji La is not especially high, and it is arguably the most consequential pass in India. It is the only road link between the Kashmir valley and Ladakh, so when it closes — commonly for four to five months — Ladakh is reachable only by air or by the longer Manali route, which closes too. The Zojila tunnel under construction is intended to make the link year-round.",history:"The pass was captured in November 1948 in an operation that used tanks at an altitude nobody had previously attempted, which decided the fate of Ladakh in the first India-Pakistan war.",caution:"The road is narrow, unstable and prone to avalanche and rockfall. It is the most accident-prone stretch on the Srinagar–Leh route."},{name:"Rohtang Pass",icon:"🚇",localName:'rohtang — "pile of corpses"',elevationM:3978,range:"pirpanjal",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"tunnel",connects:"Manali → Lahaul, and onward to Spiti and Leh",season:"Roughly May to November; the Atal Tunnel now bypasses it year-round",tagline:"Named for the people who died crossing it",desc:'Rohtang carries the road from the Kullu valley into Lahaul, and its name — commonly glossed as "pile of corpses" — records what the crossing used to cost travellers caught by weather. Since the Atal Tunnel opened beneath it in 2020, Lahaul has year-round road access for the first time, and Rohtang itself has become primarily a tourist destination rather than a necessary crossing.',history:"The historic trade and migration route between Kullu and Lahaul, and the line along which Tibetan Buddhist culture meets Hindu Kullu.",caution:"Weather changes here within minutes and has killed people who set out in clear conditions. Vehicle numbers over the pass are capped for environmental reasons."},{name:"Baralacha La",icon:"💧",localName:'bara-lacha — "pass with crossroads on summit"',elevationM:4890,range:"zanskar",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"motorable",connects:"Lahaul → Zanskar and Ladakh, on the Manali–Leh highway",season:"Roughly mid-June to late September",tagline:"A watershed between two river systems",desc:"Baralacha La sits where the Zanskar range meets the Great Himalaya, and its name refers to the routes from Zanskar, Lahaul, Spiti and Ladakh that meet on the summit plateau. It is a genuine watershed — the Bhaga rises on one side and flows to the Chenab, the Yunam on the other and flows to the Indus.",history:"A junction of four traditional trade routes, which is exactly what its name says.",caution:"Suraj Tal, just below the pass, is one of the highest lakes in India. The stretch beyond it is exposed and there is no shelter for many kilometres."},{name:"Taglang La",icon:"⛰️",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:5328,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Manali–Leh highway, above the Morey Plains",season:"Roughly mid-June to late September",tagline:"The highest point on the Manali–Leh highway",desc:"Taglang La is the highest pass on the Manali–Leh road and, like Khardung La, its signboard has historically overstated its height. It sits above the Morey Plains, a high flat expanse that is one of the more disorienting stretches of road in the Himalaya — flat ground at over 4,500 m.",history:"On the modern highway rather than a historic caravan route, which was built for military logistics after 1962.",caution:"Most travellers reach Taglang La having already crossed several 4,000 m-plus passes the same day. Cumulative altitude exposure is the risk, not the pass itself."},{name:"Chang La",icon:"🧊",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:5360,range:"ladakh",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Leh → Pangong Tso",season:"Usually open year-round, subject to snow",tagline:"The road to Pangong",desc:"Chang La carries the road from Leh east to Pangong Tso, and is among the highest motorable passes in the world by any measure. The Defence Institute of High Altitude Research operates a facility near the top, studying human physiology and agriculture at extreme altitude.",history:"Named for Changla Baba, whose small shrine stands at the summit.",caution:"Vehicles are advised not to stop for more than a short period at the top. The combination of altitude and cold makes it a poor place to linger."},{name:"Karakoram Pass",icon:"🐫",localName:'karakoram — "black gravel" (Turkic)',elevationM:5540,range:"karakoram",state:"Ladakh",status:"closed",connects:"Ladakh → Xinjiang (historically)",season:"Not open to civilian travel",tagline:"The old road to Central Asia, closed since 1949",desc:"For centuries the Karakoram Pass was the main crossing on the caravan route from Leh to Yarkand and Kashgar. The route was notorious — the approach was marked by the bones of pack animals for miles. It has been closed since 1949, and the surrounding area remains sensitive.",history:"The principal branch of the southern Silk Route through Ladakh. Its closure ended a trade that had run for well over a thousand years, and Leh's role as a caravan town ended with it.",caution:"Not accessible to civilian travellers. Included here for its historical importance rather than as a destination."},{name:"Nathu La",icon:"🤝",localName:'nathu la — "listening ears pass"',elevationM:4310,range:"eastern",state:"Sikkim",status:"motorable",connects:"Sikkim → Chumbi Valley, Tibet",season:"Generally open; trade operates seasonally",tagline:"Closed in 1962, reopened in 2006",desc:"Nathu La carried the bulk of the wool, tea and trade between India and Tibet until it was closed after the 1962 war. It reopened for limited border trade in 2006 — the first India-China trade crossing to do so — and is one of very few points where the two armies meet face to face on agreed terms.",history:"The main artery of the Kalimpong–Lhasa trade. Before 1962 a very large share of Tibet's external trade passed through this single pass.",caution:"Indian nationals need a permit; foreign nationals are generally not permitted. Rules change and should be checked locally."},{name:"Sela Pass",icon:"🛕",localName:"se la — pass",elevationM:4170,range:"eastern",state:"Arunachal Pradesh",status:"motorable",connects:"Tezpur / Dirang → Tawang",season:"Generally open; heavy snow closes it periodically",tagline:"The only road approach to Tawang",desc:"Sela is the pass every visitor to Tawang crosses, and the only road approach to the monastery town. Sela Lake sits just below the summit and is frozen for much of the winter. The Sela tunnel, opened in 2024, is intended to keep the route open when the pass itself is snowbound.",history:"Fell to Chinese forces in the 1962 war, in the action for which Jaswant Singh Rawat was decorated posthumously; a memorial to him stands nearby at Jaswantgarh.",caution:"Snow can close the pass with little warning, stranding vehicles between Dirang and Tawang."},{name:"Bum La",icon:"🚩",localName:"bum la — pass",elevationM:4633,range:"eastern",state:"Arunachal Pradesh",status:"closed",connects:"Tawang → Tsona, Tibet",season:"Restricted; permit-only day visits from Tawang",tagline:"The pass the Dalai Lama crossed in 1959",desc:"Bum La lies on the border north of Tawang. The 14th Dalai Lama crossed here into India in March 1959. It was also a main axis of the Chinese advance in 1962. It is a designated border personnel meeting point, and Indian nationals may visit on a permit issued in Tawang.",history:"A traditional route between Tawang and southern Tibet, and one of the most consequential border crossings in modern Indian history.",caution:"Permit-only, issued locally, and subject to closure at short notice."},{name:"Shipki La",icon:"🌊",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:4590,range:"great",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"closed",connects:"Kinnaur → Tibet, on the Sutlej",season:"Restricted border trade point",tagline:"Where the Sutlej enters India",desc:"Shipki La is the pass through which the Sutlej crosses from Tibet into India, and it was the traditional trade route between Kinnaur and western Tibet. It functions as a limited border trade point rather than an open crossing, and access requires an Inner Line Permit.",history:"Part of the Hindustan-Tibet road built in the nineteenth century, and the route by which Kinnauri traders reached the Tibetan markets.",caution:"Inner Line Permit required; the area is sensitive and access is discretionary."},{name:"Kunzum La",icon:"🛞",localName:"kunzum — pass",elevationM:4551,range:"great",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"motorable",connects:"Lahaul → Spiti",season:"Roughly June to October",tagline:"The gateway to Spiti",desc:"Kunzum La links Lahaul with the Spiti valley and gives access to the Chandratal lake trail. The Kunzum Devi temple at the summit is circumambulated by most vehicles crossing, a practice observed by locals and visitors alike. Beyond it the road drops into one of the driest inhabited valleys in India.",history:"The traditional link between Lahaul and Spiti, two valleys with related but distinct Buddhist cultures.",caution:"The Spiti-side road is rough and subject to washouts. Chandratal is a 9 km trek from the pass road."},{name:"Jalori Pass",icon:"🌲",localName:"jalori jot — jot means pass in Himachali",elevationM:3120,range:"pirpanjal",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"motorable",connects:"Inner Seraj → Shimla side of Kullu",season:"Open most of the year; closed after heavy snow",tagline:"A low pass with a very steep road",desc:'Jalori is one of the lowest passes in this list and among the steepest roads. It connects the Banjar valley with the Shimla side and is the access point for Serolsar Lake and the Raghupur fort ridge. The word "jot" rather than "la" marks the linguistic boundary — this is Himachali-speaking country, not Tibetan.',history:"A local rather than a trans-Himalayan route, linking valleys within Kullu district.",caution:"The gradient is severe and the road narrow. It is far more demanding to drive than its modest elevation suggests."},{name:"Chanshal Pass",icon:"🍎",localName:"chanshal — pass",elevationM:4520,range:"great",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"motorable",connects:"Rohru → Dodra Kwar, upper Shimla district",season:"Roughly June to October",tagline:"The highest pass in Shimla district",desc:"Chanshal is the highest motorable pass in Shimla district and the link to Dodra Kwar, one of the more isolated inhabited valleys in Himachal Pradesh. It is far less visited than the Lahaul and Spiti passes, and the road remains rough for much of its length.",history:"The only road access to Dodra Kwar, which was reachable only on foot until relatively recently.",caution:"Limited fuel and services beyond Rohru. The pass closes early in the season compared with lower crossings."},{name:"Fotu La",icon:"⛰️",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:4108,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Kargil → Leh, on NH-1",season:"Usually open year-round",tagline:"The highest point on the Srinagar–Leh highway",desc:'Fotu La is the highest pass on the Srinagar–Leh road, higher than Zoji La though far less troublesome, since it lies in the rain shadow and receives much less snow. The Lamayuru "moonland" formations — eroded lacustrine sediments — are just below it.',history:"On the historic route between Kashmir and Ladakh, past the Lamayuru monastery, one of the oldest in Ladakh.",caution:"Generally straightforward, but it is reached after Zoji La, which is the difficult part of the route."},{name:"Namika La",icon:"🗿",localName:'namika la — "pillar of the sky pass"',elevationM:3700,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Kargil → Leh, on NH-1",season:"Usually open year-round",tagline:"The pillar of the sky",desc:"Namika La sits between Kargil and Fotu La on the Srinagar–Leh road. Its name refers to the rock pillar visible near the summit. It marks the transition from the greener Kargil side into the arid Ladakhi landscape proper.",history:"On the Kashmir–Ladakh caravan route, and part of the traditional staging pattern between Kargil and Lamayuru.",caution:""},{name:"Marsimik La",icon:"❄️",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:5582,range:"ladakh",state:"Ladakh",status:"closed",connects:"Pangong region → the Chang Chenmo valley",season:"Restricted; not generally open to civilian traffic",tagline:"Once claimed as the world's highest motorable pass",desc:"Marsimik La at roughly 5,582 m was for years put forward as the highest motorable pass in the world, before Umling La settled the question. It lies in a sensitive area near the Line of Actual Control and is not generally open to civilian travel.",history:"A traditional grazing route for Changpa herders into the Chang Chenmo.",caution:"Access is restricted and the track is rough even where permitted."},{name:"Shingo La",icon:"🥾",localName:"shinkun la — pass",elevationM:5091,range:"zanskar",state:"Himachal Pradesh / Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Lahaul (Darcha) → Zanskar (Padum)",season:"Roughly July to September",tagline:"The new road link into Zanskar",desc:"Shingo La is the crossing on the Darcha–Padum road, which gives Zanskar a second road access and shortens the journey substantially compared with the Kargil route. It was a well-known trekking pass long before it became motorable, and the Shinku La tunnel is planned to keep the link open through winter.",history:"A traditional trekking and trading route between Lahaul and Zanskar, walked for generations before any road existed.",caution:"The road is new, rough, and crosses several unbridged streams that rise sharply through the day as snowmelt increases."},{name:"Pensi La",icon:"🏔️",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:4400,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Kargil (Suru valley) → Zanskar (Padum)",season:"Roughly June to October",tagline:"The gateway to Zanskar, beside the Drang-Drung glacier",desc:"Pensi La is the traditional road entry to Zanskar from the Suru valley. The Drang-Drung glacier, one of the largest in Ladakh outside the Siachen system, is visible from the pass — an unusually accessible view of a major glacier from a road.",history:"The historic route between Kargil and Zanskar, and until the Shingo La road, the only vehicle access.",caution:"The Zanskar road beyond the pass is long and remote, with very limited services."},{name:"Lipulekh Pass",icon:"🛕",localName:"lipulekh — pass",elevationM:5115,range:"great",state:"Uttarakhand",status:"closed",connects:"Kumaon → Taklakot, Tibet",season:"Restricted; used for the Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra",tagline:"The pilgrimage route to Kailash",desc:"Lipulekh is the traditional route from Kumaon to Taklakot in Tibet and the classic Indian approach to Kailash and Mansarovar. It remains one of the designated border trade points with China and is used for the government-organised Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra. The area is also subject to a boundary claim by Nepal.",history:"A pilgrimage and trade route of great antiquity, used by Bhotiya traders and by pilgrims heading for Kailash.",caution:"Access is restricted and organised; independent travel is not permitted."},{name:"Mana Pass",icon:"🛕",localName:"mana — pass",elevationM:5545,range:"great",state:"Uttarakhand",status:"closed",connects:"Badrinath / Mana village → Tibet",season:"Restricted",tagline:"Above the last village on the Indian side",desc:"Mana Pass lies beyond Mana village near Badrinath, which is marked as the last Indian village on that road. The pass carried trade between Garhwal and Tibet before 1962. A road has been built to near the top for military purposes, but the crossing itself is not open.",history:"The Garhwal–Tibet trade route, and part of the pilgrimage geography around Badrinath.",caution:"Restricted area. Mana village itself is accessible; the pass is not."},{name:"Niti Pass",icon:"⛰️",localName:"niti — pass",elevationM:5068,range:"great",state:"Uttarakhand",status:"closed",connects:"Niti valley → Tibet",season:"Restricted",tagline:"A Garhwal–Tibet route closed since 1962",desc:"Niti Pass was one of the main routes between Garhwal and western Tibet, used by Bhotiya traders who moved seasonally between the two. It closed after 1962, and the trading communities of the Niti valley lost the economy their settlement pattern had been built around.",history:"The closure of Niti and Mana ended a transhumant trading economy that had operated for centuries.",caution:"Inner Line Permit required to travel far up the Niti valley; the pass is closed."},{name:"Bara-lacha to Sarchu: Nakee La",icon:"🌀",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:4739,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Sarchu → Pang, on the Manali–Leh highway",season:"Roughly mid-June to late September",tagline:"The first of the Gata Loops passes",desc:"Nakee La is reached by the Gata Loops, twenty-one hairpin bends climbing out of the Sarchu plain. It is followed almost immediately by Lachulung La, making this the most concentrated stretch of altitude gain on the Manali–Leh road.",history:"On the modern highway rather than a historic route.",caution:"Sarchu, where most travellers camp before this section, is itself at about 4,290 m and is a common place to develop altitude sickness."},{name:"Lachulung La",icon:"🪨",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:5059,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Nakee La → Pang, on the Manali–Leh highway",season:"Roughly mid-June to late September",tagline:"Second of the back-to-back highway passes",desc:"Lachulung La follows Nakee La within a few kilometres, and the descent from it runs through the eroded canyon formations above Pang. The two passes together are where most travellers on the Manali–Leh road first feel the altitude properly.",history:"On the modern highway.",caution:"Pang, below the pass, has one of the highest transit camps in the world and is a frequent site of altitude illness."},{name:"Wari La",icon:"🛤️",localName:"la — pass",elevationM:5312,range:"ladakh",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Nubra (Agham) → Sakti, near Leh",season:"Roughly May to October",tagline:"The quiet alternative to Khardung La",desc:"Wari La is an alternative crossing between the Nubra valley and the Indus valley, avoiding Khardung La entirely. It is barely used by comparison, and the road is rougher, which is precisely why it appeals to people who want the crossing without the traffic.",history:"A local route between Nubra and the Sakti area.",caution:"Very little traffic means very little help if something goes wrong. Fuel and phone coverage are both absent for long stretches."},{name:"Hampta Pass",icon:"🥾",localName:"hampta — pass",elevationM:4270,range:"pirpanjal",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"trek",connects:"Kullu valley → Lahaul (Chandra valley)",season:"Roughly June to early October",tagline:"Green on one side, desert on the other",desc:"Hampta is a trekking pass rather than a road crossing, and it is the clearest illustration in the region of what a Himalayan crossing does: the Kullu side is forested and wet, the Lahaul side is bare and dry. The pass is the rain barrier, and you walk from one climate to the other in a day.",history:"A shepherd and trading route between Kullu and Lahaul, used long before the Rohtang road.",caution:"The Lahaul-side descent involves stream crossings that vary considerably with the time of day and the season."},{name:"Pin Parvati Pass",icon:"🥾",localName:"pin parvati — pass",elevationM:5319,range:"great",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"trek",connects:"Parvati valley (Kullu) → Pin valley (Spiti)",season:"Roughly July to September",tagline:"One of the hardest trekking crossings in Himachal",desc:"Pin Parvati links the wet, forested Parvati valley with the arid Pin valley in Spiti, crossing a glaciated col at over 5,300 m. It is a serious high-altitude trek requiring glacier travel, and it is regularly rated among the more demanding standard routes in the Indian Himalaya.",history:"A traditional shepherd crossing between Kullu and Spiti, now used almost entirely by trekkers.",caution:"Crevassed glacier terrain and a long committing descent. Not a route to attempt without experience and a competent guide."},{name:"Auden's Col",icon:"🧗",localName:"named for John Auden, Geological Survey of India",elevationM:5490,range:"great",state:"Uttarakhand",status:"trek",connects:"Gangotri glacier system → Rudragaira and the Bhilangana",season:"Roughly late August to September",tagline:"A technical col, not a walking pass",desc:"Auden's Col crosses between the Jogin and Gangotri massifs and is named for the geologist John Bicknell Auden, who identified it in 1935 — brother of the poet W. H. Auden. It requires roped glacier travel and is closer to a mountaineering objective than a trekking route.",history:"Surveyed by the Geological Survey of India in the 1930s.",caution:"Technical mountaineering terrain with objective hazard. It has claimed lives, including on organised expeditions."},{name:"Traill's Pass",icon:"🧗",localName:"named for George William Traill, Kumaon Commissioner",elevationM:5312,range:"great",state:"Uttarakhand",status:"trek",connects:"Pindari glacier → Milam valley",season:"Roughly September",tagline:"Crossed in 1830, and still rarely repeated",desc:"Traill's Pass crosses the ridge between Nanda Devi and Nanda Kot, connecting the Pindari and Milam valleys. George William Traill crossed it in 1830, and it has been repeated relatively few times since. It is one of the more serious non-technical-grade crossings in the Indian Himalaya.",history:"A traditional route between the Pindar and Gori valleys, known to local shepherds before Traill recorded it.",caution:"Steep, exposed and objectively hazardous on the Milam side. Expedition-grade."},{name:"Nathang / Jelep La",icon:"🐎",localName:'jelep la — "the lovely level pass"',elevationM:4267,range:"eastern",state:"Sikkim",status:"closed",connects:"Sikkim → Chumbi Valley, Tibet",season:"Closed to civilians",tagline:"The other Sikkim trade pass, still shut",desc:'Jelep La carried a large share of the Kalimpong–Lhasa trade alongside Nathu La, and was the route of the Younghusband expedition in 1903-04. Unlike Nathu La it has not reopened, and remains closed. Its name translates roughly as "the lovely level pass", which says something about how it compared with the alternatives.',history:"The main route of the British Tibet trade and of the 1903-04 mission to Lhasa.",caution:"Closed to civilian travel; the road to the general area requires permits."},{name:"Dungri La (Sach Pass)",icon:"🪨",localName:"sach — pass",elevationM:4420,range:"pirpanjal",state:"Himachal Pradesh",status:"motorable",connects:"Chamba → Pangi valley",season:"Roughly late June to October",tagline:"One of the most demanding roads in Himachal",desc:"Sach Pass connects Chamba with the isolated Pangi valley on the Chenab. The road is narrow, unsurfaced for long stretches, and crosses several unbridged snowmelt streams. It has a considerable reputation among motorcyclists, and the reputation is deserved.",history:"The traditional link between Chamba and Pangi, which was otherwise reachable only via Lahaul.",caution:"Loose surface, sheer drops and stream crossings that deepen through the afternoon. Cross early in the day."},{name:"Chorten Nyima La",icon:"🏳️",localName:'chorten nyima — "sun stupa"',elevationM:5470,range:"eastern",state:"Sikkim",status:"closed",connects:"North Sikkim → Tibet",season:"Closed",tagline:"A high northern Sikkim crossing",desc:"Chorten Nyima La lies on the northern Sikkim border and takes its name from a nearby pilgrimage site. It is closed and lies deep within a restricted zone, and is included here to show that the Sikkim frontier has more crossings than the two that get discussed.",history:"A pilgrimage and grazing route rather than a major trade artery.",caution:"Restricted area; not accessible."},{name:"Tanglang alternatives: Debring",icon:"🌬️",localName:"debring — plain and junction",elevationM:4600,range:"zanskar",state:"Ladakh",status:"motorable",connects:"Morey Plains junction → Tso Kar and Manali–Leh road",season:"Roughly mid-June to late September",tagline:"A junction rather than a col, and the wind is the point",desc:"Debring is a road junction on the edge of the Morey Plains rather than a pass in the strict sense, but it is where the Tso Kar road leaves the highway and it is one of the windiest inhabited points on the route. It is included as a reminder that at these altitudes the exposure between passes can be harder than the passes.",history:"A nomad camping ground on the Rupshu plateau, used by Changpa herders.",caution:"Sustained high wind and no shelter. Not somewhere to break down."}],MP_NAMES=[{term:"La",language:"Tibetan, Ladakhi, Sikkimese",note:'The standard word for a pass across the Tibetan cultural sphere. Khardung La, Zoji La, Nathu La — the word is already in the name, so "Nathu La pass" is redundant.'},{term:"Jot",language:"Himachali (Pahari)",note:"Used across Chamba, Kullu and Mandi. Jalori Jot, Sach Jot. Marks the boundary of Tibetan naming."},{term:"Ghati / Ghat",language:"Hindi, Sanskrit-derived",note:"A pass or defile, used more in the lower ranges and in the peninsula than in the high Himalaya."},{term:"Dhar / Dhaar",language:"Pahari",note:"A ridge or ridge-crossing rather than a col proper. Common in Uttarakhand and Himachal."},{term:"Khal",language:"Garhwali, Kumaoni",note:"A pass or saddle. Appears in place names across the Uttarakhand hills."},{term:"Col",language:"English / French, via mountaineering",note:"The technical term for the low point on a ridge between two peaks. Used for crossings without a local name, such as Auden's Col."},{term:"Bhanjyang",language:"Nepali, used in Sikkim and Darjeeling",note:"A saddle or pass, common in the eastern Himalayan foothills."}],MP_ROUTES=[{icon:"🐫",title:"Leh–Yarkand caravan route",era:"Antiquity to 1949",desc:"The southern branch of the Silk Route, crossing the Karakoram Pass to Yarkand and Kashgar. Pashmina wool moved south, textiles and precious goods north. The route was brutal — the approach to the Karakoram Pass was marked for miles by the bones of pack animals. Its closure in 1949 ended Leh's role as a caravan town."},{icon:"🍵",title:"Kalimpong–Lhasa wool road",era:"c. 1900 to 1962",desc:"Over Nathu La and Jelep La into the Chumbi valley. At its height this carried a very large share of Tibet's external trade, and Kalimpong grew into a substantial entrepôt on the strength of it. The 1962 war closed it, and Kalimpong has never recovered the position."},{icon:"🧂",title:"Kinnaur–Tibet salt and grain trade",era:"Antiquity to 1962",desc:"Over Shipki La along the Sutlej, with Kinnauri traders exchanging grain and manufactured goods for Tibetan salt, wool and borax. The Hindustan-Tibet road was built along this line in the nineteenth century."},{icon:"🛕",title:"Garhwal–Tibet Bhotiya trade",era:"Antiquity to 1962",desc:"Over Mana, Niti and Lipulekh. The Bhotiya communities of the upper Garhwal and Kumaon valleys moved seasonally between Indian and Tibetan markets — an entire settlement pattern built around the crossing. 1962 ended it, and the villages emptied."},{icon:"🐑",title:"Lahaul–Zanskar–Ladakh routes",era:"Antiquity to present",desc:"Over Shingo La, Baralacha La and Pensi La. Primarily internal rather than international — grain, salt, wool and livestock moving between the high valleys. These are the routes that have survived, because they did not depend on a border staying open."},{icon:"🏔️",title:"Kashmir–Ladakh route",era:"Antiquity to present",desc:"Over Zoji La, Namika La and Fotu La. The line the modern NH-1 follows, and the reason Kargil, Lamayuru and Leh sit where they do. It has been continuously used for longer than any other route on this list."}],MP_TUNNELS=[{icon:"🚇",name:"Atal Tunnel",year:"2020",length:"9.02 km",pass:"Under Rohtang Pass",desc:"Gives Lahaul year-round road access for the first time and cuts roughly 46 km from the Manali–Keylong journey. Among the longest highway tunnels above 3,000 m anywhere."},{icon:"🚇",name:"Sela Tunnel",year:"2024",length:"~1.6 km (twin tubes)",pass:"Below Sela Pass",desc:"Keeps the Tawang road open when Sela itself is snowbound, and removes the highest and most weather-exposed section of the approach."},{icon:"🚧",name:"Zojila Tunnel",year:"Under construction",length:"~13 km",pass:"Under Zoji La",desc:"Intended to give Ladakh year-round road access from Kashmir. It would be among the longest road tunnels in Asia and would end the annual four-to-five-month closure."},{icon:"🚧",name:"Shinku La Tunnel",year:"Under construction",length:"~4.1 km",pass:"Under Shingo La",desc:"Would keep the Darcha–Padum road open through winter, giving Zanskar its first year-round link."}],MP_SAFETY=[{icon:"🧠",title:"Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS)",severity:"Common",desc:"Headache with nausea, fatigue, dizziness or poor sleep, typically appearing 6–12 hours after arriving at altitude. It is common above 3,000 m and is the body signalling that ascent has outpaced adaptation. The response is to stop ascending — not to push on and hope."},{icon:"🫁",title:"High Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE)",severity:"Life-threatening",desc:"Fluid in the lungs. Breathlessness at rest, a persistent cough, and a feeling of drowning. It can develop within hours and kills. The only reliable treatment is immediate descent."},{icon:"🧠",title:"High Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE)",severity:"Life-threatening",desc:"Fluid on the brain. Confusion, loss of coordination, behaviour that is out of character. Someone with HACE frequently cannot judge their own condition, so the decision has to be made by whoever is with them. Descend immediately."},{icon:"📈",title:"Acclimatisation",severity:"Prevention",desc:"Above 3,000 m, the standard guidance is to raise sleeping altitude by no more than 300–500 m per day and to take a rest day every 3–4 days. Climbing high and sleeping low is the principle. Fitness offers no protection whatsoever — fit people get altitude sickness too."},{icon:"🚗",title:"The specific risk of driving",severity:"High",desc:"Flying into Leh, or driving from Manali or Srinagar, puts people above 3,500 m within hours — far faster than any trekker would ascend. Nearly every serious altitude incident in Ladakh follows this pattern. Two rest days in Leh before crossing anything high is the standard advice for a reason."},{icon:"💧",title:"Cold, dehydration and sun",severity:"Moderate",desc:"Dry air at altitude causes fluid loss faster than most people notice, dehydration worsens AMS symptoms, and UV exposure is far higher than at sea level. Snow blindness is a real risk on a snow-covered pass without eye protection."}],MP_PERMITS=[{icon:"📄",title:"Inner Line Permit (ILP)",desc:"Required for Indian nationals travelling to specified areas near the border — parts of Ladakh, Kinnaur, Spiti, Sikkim, Arunachal Pradesh, Nagaland, Mizoram and Manipur. Usually obtained online or from the district administration, and often checked at more than one point along the route."},{icon:"🌏",title:"Protected Area Permit (PAP)",desc:"Required for foreign nationals in certain border areas. Conditions are more restrictive than the ILP and frequently include a minimum group size and travel through a registered agent. Some areas are barred to foreign nationals entirely."},{icon:"📍",title:"Where they apply",desc:"Pangong, Tso Moriri, Nubra, Dahanu, Nathu La, Tawang, Upper Kinnaur toward Shipki La and much of the Arunachal frontier all require permits. Requirements are set locally and change with conditions on the ground."},{icon:"⚠️",title:"Treat this page as background only",desc:"Permit rules, fees and eligible areas change, sometimes at short notice. Nothing here should be relied on for planning. Check with the district administration or a licensed local operator before travelling."}],MP_GALLERY=[{icon:"🏳️",title:"Prayer Flags",caption:"Strung across almost every la in the Tibetan cultural sphere."},{icon:"🐫",title:"The Caravan Road",caption:"Leh to Yarkand over the Karakoram Pass, closed since 1949."},{icon:"🚇",title:"Atal Tunnel",caption:"Nine kilometres under Rohtang, and Lahaul stopped being cut off."},{icon:"❄️",title:"Zoji La in Winter",caption:"Four to five months closed, and Ladakh reachable only by air."},{icon:"🌧️",title:"The Rain Barrier",caption:"At Hampta you walk from forest to desert in a single day."},{icon:"📏",title:"Disputed Elevations",caption:"Signboards and surveys frequently disagree by a hundred metres or more."},{icon:"🛕",title:"Sela and Tawang",caption:"One pass, and the only road to the monastery beyond it."},{icon:"🧊",title:"Drang-Drung",caption:"A major glacier visible from the road at Pensi La."},{icon:"🚩",title:"Bum La",caption:"Crossed by the Dalai Lama in March 1959."},{icon:"🫁",title:"Thin Air",caption:"At Umling La there is roughly half the oxygen there is at sea level."}],MP_FACTS=['La means pass in Tibetan, Ladakhi and Sikkimese. "Khardung La pass" therefore says pass twice — the word is already in the name.',"Umling La, completed in 2021 at roughly 5,798 m, is the highest motorable road in the world. It is higher than Everest Base Camp on either the Nepal or the Tibet side.",'The "world\'s highest motorable road" board at Khardung La claimed 18,380 ft for decades. Survey work puts the pass nearer 5,359 m, and several Ladakhi passes are higher.','Rohtang means, by the usual gloss, "pile of corpses" — a record of what the crossing cost travellers caught out by the weather.',"Zoji La is only about 3,528 m, far lower than most passes on this page, and it is the most consequential: when it closes, Ladakh loses its road link to Kashmir for four to five months.","In November 1948 Indian forces took Zoji La using tanks at an altitude at which nobody had previously attempted armoured operations. It decided the fate of Ladakh.","The Karakoram Pass was the main route between Ladakh and Central Asia for well over a thousand years. It has been closed since 1949, and Leh's role as a caravan town ended with it.","Nathu La carried a very large share of Tibet's external trade until 1962. It reopened for limited border trade in 2006, the first India-China trade crossing to do so.","The 14th Dalai Lama entered India over Bum La in Arunachal Pradesh in March 1959.","The Atal Tunnel, opened in 2020 beneath Rohtang, gave Lahaul year-round road access for the first time in its history.","Auden's Col is named for the geologist John Bicknell Auden of the Geological Survey of India — the brother of the poet W. H. Auden.","Traill's Pass between the Pindari and Milam valleys was first crossed in 1830 by George William Traill and has been repeated relatively few times since.","The word for a pass changes with the language, not the altitude: la in Ladakh and Sikkim, jot in Himachal, khal in Garhwal and Kumaon.","Hampta Pass is the clearest demonstration of what a Himalayan pass does — the Kullu side is wet forest, the Lahaul side is high desert, and the walk between them takes a day."],MP_QUIZ=[{q:'What does "la" mean in Khardung La, Zoji La and Nathu La?',options:["Mountain","Pass","Lake","Border"],answer:1,explain:'It is the Tibetan, Ladakhi and Sikkimese word for a pass — so "Nathu La pass" says pass twice.'},{q:"Which is the highest motorable road in the world?",options:["Khardung La","Umling La","Marsimik La","Chang La"],answer:1,explain:"Umling La in eastern Ladakh, at roughly 5,798 m, completed by the Border Roads Organisation in 2021. The older claim for Khardung La did not survive proper survey."},{q:"Why is Zoji La so important despite being one of the lower passes?",options:["It is the highest point on the Manali–Leh highway","It is the only road link between Kashmir and Ladakh, and closes for four to five months a year","It is the main India–China trade crossing","It is the source of the Indus"],answer:1,explain:"When Zoji La closes, Ladakh loses its road connection to Kashmir. The Zojila tunnel is being built specifically to end that annual closure."},{q:"What does the name Rohtang mean?",options:["Pass of the winds","Pile of corpses","Gateway of snow","Meeting of valleys"],answer:1,explain:'The usual gloss is "pile of corpses", recording how dangerous the crossing was for travellers caught by weather.'},{q:"Which pass did the 14th Dalai Lama cross into India in 1959?",options:["Nathu La","Bum La","Shipki La","Lipulekh"],answer:1,explain:"Bum La, north of Tawang in Arunachal Pradesh, in March 1959."},{q:"What ended the Leh–Yarkand caravan trade over the Karakoram Pass?",options:["The 1962 war","The closure of the route around 1949","The opening of the Manali–Leh highway","A glacier advance blocking the pass"],answer:1,explain:"The route closed around 1949 and has not reopened. Leh's function as a caravan town ended with it."},{q:"What is the correct first response to suspected HAPE or HACE?",options:["Rest at the same altitude and monitor","Descend immediately","Drink more water and continue slowly","Ascend to a hut with oxygen"],answer:1,explain:"Immediate descent. Both are life-threatening and can develop within hours; waiting at altitude to see whether it improves is how people die."},{q:'What does the Himachali word "jot" mean?',options:["A ridge camp","A pass","A glacier","A mountain lake"],answer:1,explain:"Jot is the Pahari word for a pass — Jalori Jot, Sach Jot. It marks the boundary of Tibetan naming in the western Himalaya."},{q:"Which tunnel gave Lahaul year-round road access for the first time?",options:["Zojila Tunnel","Atal Tunnel","Sela Tunnel","Shinku La Tunnel"],answer:1,explain:"The Atal Tunnel, opened in 2020 beneath Rohtang Pass, about 9 km long."},{q:"Why do sources disagree about the elevation of a pass?",options:["Because passes physically change height each year",'Because "height" may mean the road summit or the geographic col, and different surveys were used',"Because measurements are taken in different seasons","Because elevation is measured from the nearest town"],answer:1,explain:"Road summit and geographic col are not the same point, and older figures often came from less accurate survey methods. Differences of 50–150 m for the same pass are routine."},{q:"What is an Inner Line Permit for?",options:["Foreign nationals visiting any part of India","Indian nationals travelling to specified areas near the border","Permission to trek above 5,000 m","Driving a private vehicle in Ladakh"],answer:1,explain:"The ILP applies to Indian nationals in specified border areas. Foreign nationals need a Protected Area Permit, which is more restrictive, and some areas are barred to them entirely."},{q:"What does the Hampta Pass crossing demonstrate particularly clearly?",options:["The effect of altitude on vehicle engines","That the range is a rain barrier — forest on one side, high desert on the other","The location of the Indo-Tibetan border","The extent of glacier retreat"],answer:1,explain:"The Kullu side is wet and forested; the Lahaul side is arid. You cross from one climate to the other in a single day's walk."}];